    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "837beecf-4239-4b3d-b683-1d611af3da2e",
      "key": "66a5fe0a-9fac-4639-93bb-356cc3475eb1",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Itinerary"
        },
        {
          "alias": "richTextContent",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Ch3\u003EDay 1\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe depart early from our designated pick up points this morning and travel by executive coach to Heysham. On arrival at Heysham we board our Isle of Man Steam Packet ferry crossing to Douglas. On arrival at Douglas we continue to the 3* Ramsey Park Hotel at Ramsey where we stay five nights on a half board basis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 2\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe firstly enjoy some time at leisure in Ramsey. We then enjoy a scenic excursion from Ramsey to Laxey on the Manx Electric Railway, one of Britain\u2019s longest vintage narrow gauge railways. At Laxey, we explore the Great Laxey Wheel. Dating back to 1854 and known locally as \u2018Lady Isabella\u2019 after the wife of the island\u2019s governor at that time, it is the largest water wheel in the world and was designed by Robert Casement to pump water from the mineshafts. We then board the Snaefell Mountain Railway up to the summit of Snaefell.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 3\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis morning we take a ride on the Douglas Horse Trams. The trams were first introduced in 1876 to take advantage of the booming tourist trade in Victorian times and to this day still carry tourists and locals along the promenades. We then visit the Manx Museum which contemplates the natural history, archaeology and social development of the island. Our guided tour of the Gaiety Theatre this afternoon covers the auditorium, the backstage areas and the history of this iconic venue.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 4\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECregneash Village Folk Museum provides a living, working illustration of the traditional customs, crafts and skills which characterised the Manx crofter\u2019s way of life. After free time for lunch at the old Manx capital of Castletown, we explore Castle Rushen. Guarding the entrance to Silverburn River and one of England\u0026#39;s best preserved medieval fortresses, the castle was taken by Robert the Bruce in 1313 who occupied the Isle of Man briefly whilst attempting to invade Ireland. The Castletown Steam Railway, opened in 1874 and still running with its original locomotives and the longest line of its type in Britain, takes us from Castletown to Douglas.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 5\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis morning we visit Peel, one of the world\u2019s smallest cathedral cities with just 3,800 residents. On arrival we see the remains of Peel Castle, occupying the important site of St Patrick\u2019s Isle at Peel. The castle\u2019s curtain wall encircles the ruins of many buildings which are a testimony to the site\u2019s religious and secular importance in Manx history. We then visit Peel Cathedral, consecrated in 1980. Our visit to Peel concludes with a visit to The House of Manannan heritage visitor centre, where, the Island\u2019s mythological sea god, Manannan, welcomes you to his fascinating Kingdom. Later this afternoon we visit the Milntown Estate House. During our guided tour we see elegant rooms and learn about the powerful Christian dynasty, who occupied Milntown for 500 years, and the vision of Sir Clive Edwards, who left the Estate in trust for the benefit of the Manx people.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 6\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe return to Douglas Port for our Isle of Man Steam Packet ferry crossing to Heysham. Taking carefully selected comfort stops en route, we return back to our original pick up points.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureDescription",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": ""
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureSummary",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003ENB:- Please note we reserve the right to alter the above itinerary if required for operational\nreasons. Given the historic nature of the places visited, a certain degree of walking will be involved, and this holiday may not be suitable for those with mobility problems.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
